-- Q5015: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 450.A7, the iron curtain approach evaluates the cumulative balance sheet effect ($320,000 exceeds $250,000 materiality), while the rollover approach evaluates only the current-year originating amount ($80,000, below $175,000 performance materiality). SAB 108 requires dual evaluation using both methods.
Wrong (A): The two approaches do not yield the same conclusion. The iron curtain method flags the misstatement as material while the rollover method does not.
Wrong (C): The rollover approach is actually less conservative here because it only captures the current-period origination ($80,000), ignoring the cumulative $320,000 balance sheet effect.
Wrong (D): The iron curtain approach clearly identifies the $320,000 cumulative misstatement as material. Estimation uncertainty does not exempt the misstatement from evaluation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5015;

-- Q5016: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 265.12, the auditor must communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance no later than 60 days after the audit report release date. This deadline is tied specifically to the report release date.
Wrong (A): The standard specifies 60 days, not 30 days. A 30-day deadline is not established by AU-C 265 for this communication.
Wrong (B): While earlier communication is encouraged, AU-C 265 does not require the written communication before the audit report is issued.
Wrong (C): The deadline is measured from the audit report release date, not from the end of the fiscal year. These are different reference points.$EXPL$
